d "aa25d7b1-96fa-52f0-be81-b199d162aff1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102700 #edb/ref 1080 "15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102700 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102700 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ae67b8ec-458e-55d4-b5a0-2d5265868380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102701 #edb/ref 1080 "18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102701 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102701 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01d4e827-5964-5226-ade4-d2fe911613ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102702 #edb/ref 1080 "24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102702 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102702 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26f4048a-e58a-5660-9d0c-02d705d513f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102703 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102703 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102703 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b07a6aa-ddb4-5bbb-a063-0274c04df652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105528 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105528 #edb/ref 1071 "The perimeter $p_1$ of the triangle $\\triangle{ABC}$ shown below is $21.$ What is the perimeter of the triangle $\\triangle{ABD}$ if ${BD}=3?$\n\n![](images/66/66780ce63515be8445234ce991ae64506c6325f04b2aabb0318fb8b86c1bf2d7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00942425-ab13-5a50-a63a-d29cf2f34889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105528 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagram, we can see that $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an equilateral triangle.\n\nWe're told that the perimeter of $\\triangle ABC$ is $p_1=21,$ and since all $3$ sides have equal length, each side must have a length of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p_1}{3}= \\dfrac{21}{3} = 7\\,.\n$$\n\nSo now we know that $AB=BC=AC = 7,$ and we have enough information to compute the perimeter of the triangle $\\triangle{ABD}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BD+DA \\\\  & =7+3+7 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of $\\triangle ABD$ is $p=17.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105529 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105529 #edb/ref 1071 "In a pentagon $ABCDE$, the measures of the sides are ${AB} = 25\\,\\mathrm{mm},$ ${BC} = 26\\,\\mathrm{mm},$ ${CD} = 30\\,\\mathrm{mm},$ ${DE} = 18\\,\\mathrm{mm},$ and ${EA} = 21\\,\\mathrm{mm}.$\nWhat is the perimeter of the pentagon?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4864184-6983-58ab-9787-524e04dc5c73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105529 #edb/ref 1074 "The perimeter $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =25\\,mm+26\\,mm+30\\,mm+18\\,mm+21\\,mm \\\\  & =120\\,mm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is a large quantity of $\\textrm{mm},$ we will convert to $\\textrm{cm}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\np = 120\\, \\mathrm{mm} = 12\\,\\mathrm{cm}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105530 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105530 #edb/ref 1071 "A regular pentagon has the same perimeter as an equilateral triangle whose side is $15\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$ Find the length of the side of the pentagon." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3de76784-4137-5093-9c35-847edd10ef62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105530 #edb/ref 1074 "Since all the sides of an equilateral triangle are equal, its perimeter is \n\n$$\np=3\\cdot 15=45.\n$$\n\nLet $a$ be the side of the pentagon. We know that our polygons have equal perimeters. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =5a \\\\ 45 & =5a \\\\ 9 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the side is $9 \\,\\mathrm{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105806 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105806 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105806 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105806 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Perimeter of a Triangle Given the Perimeter of Another Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105806 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105806 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b1d4b0c2-10d0-5c3a-8a58-4d7598dd9f96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105807 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105807 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Perimeter of a Polygon" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105807 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105807 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6f6c0ca1-297a-5f25-bccf-c74d0c565c24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105808 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105808 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105808 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Value of Variables Given Two Isoperimetric Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105808 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105808 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b2c2a15f-617d-5515-98a8-ccdc525092b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1045 "The Perimeter of a Polygon" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106009 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2fb56d40-545a-510d-a693-25bf992fdb77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106137 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4a/4ab3c4ba281be5363c5ea3e2b3fce6aec91221b31a762ef645c7262095866d5e.png)\n\nThe perimeter ${p}_{1}$ of the triangle $△DBC$ shown above is $10.$ What is the perimeter of the triangle $△ABC$ if $BD=3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96a7a6cd-52f8-51e5-9286-b855874bfcac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106137 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagram, we can see that $△ABC$ is an equilateral triangle and $△DBC$ is an isosceles triangle with $CD=DB.$\n\nWe're told that the perimeter of $△DBC$ is ${p}_{1}=10,$ and $DB=3,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1} & =DB+BC+CD \\\\ 10 & =3+BC+3 \\\\ 4 & =BC.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo now we have enough information to compute the perimeter of the triangle $△ABC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =3BC \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of $△ABC$ is $p=12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106737 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106737 #edb/ref 1061 623181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2dd79ef0-aa4a-5d52-ba67-92d8d47fd909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106738 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106738 #edb/ref 1061 623182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48b5c886-bff4-52b2-b702-6be7c990ade1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106739 #edb/ref 1061 623185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2154c4c2-efe1-5888-87e0-905e612555d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1061 623186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2896eab9-aca7-5163-8e08-2101c4fa2827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1061 623189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffc5ce2a-4bcb-5660-a1ce-e79bd1b4c604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106742 #edb/ref 1061 623190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec3bbafa-69db-5676-8013-221157e979a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46a85010d46969ffb7df440efd72bb3fa8d1394b35cc2887ee4567cda15079c5.png)\n\nThe sum of the length of its sides is called the **perimeter**. Here, the perimeter of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\  & =2+4.2+3.8 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1065 2244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7664d7c3-e47c-57b6-97aa-1131f036852c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a quadrilateral $ABCD$ as shown below and it is known that ${AD} = 6\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${AB} = 2\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${BC} = 4\\,\\mathrm{m},$ and $CD=5\\,\\mathrm{m}.$ What is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?   \n\n![](images/a7/a74251f1c72eccad42b98e0d3fd9a7e7cd0a60018a6f03bb65ec2abc34eb1c88.png)\n\nRemember that the **perimeter** $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AD+AB+BC+CD \\\\  & =6+2+4+5 \\\\  & =17\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1065 2246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0428807f-e6a6-5280-81bc-ab454203aebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1067 "Perimeters of Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the polygons depicted below and find their perimeters.\n\n![](images/69/69ef06a71f13625ad4525a03966f4a4c62ae2b2136b085e97b8dc02a6a6963cc.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we conclude that polygons I, II and III all have a perimeter of $10$ while polygon IV has a perimeter of $8.$ \n\nPolygons that have the same perimeter are called **isoperimetric**. So I, II and III are isoperimetric polygons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1065 2247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "596db00e-9cf7-51e1-902e-73c6670780eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1067 "Isoperimetric Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491502 #edb/ref 1080 "40\\,mm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491502 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759218